---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { supportContact } from '../config';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Help"
  description="How to use plank.to: first-run setup, tasks, weekly routines, optional reminders, weather and location controls, clothing preferences, and how to send feedback."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Help</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      plank.to is a daily planner made for autistic people, people with ADHD, and anyone who wants a
      clear, predictable day. Everything is optional except the things you write down yourself. This
      page explains each part in plain language.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <h2 id="toc-heading">On this page</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="#first-run">First-run setup</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#tasks">Tasks</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#routines">Weekly routines</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#reminders">Optional reminders</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#weather">Weather and location</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#clothing">Clothing preferences</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#appearance">Text size and colors</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#feedback">Ideas and problems</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="first-run">First-run setup</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account to create and no sign-in. When you open the app you land on{' '}
      <strong>Today</strong>, which is ready to use straight away. The app has two tabs:{' '}
      <strong>Today</strong> and
      <strong>Settings</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your tasks and routines are saved on your device, and the app works without a network. Your
        phone's own backup (iCloud or Google) may also keep a copy, so they can come back if you
        move to a new phone. See the <a href="/privacy/#backups">privacy page</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reminders and weather are both off until you choose them. The app asks for notification
        permission only after you tap "Turn on reminders", and asks for location permission only
        after you turn on weather and pick "Use my device location".
      </li>
      <li>
        If you are not ready to decide about reminders, tap "Not now" on the reminders card. It does
        not keep coming back, and you can turn reminders on later in Settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can change text size, spacing, and colors in Settings at any time. See
        <a href="#appearance">Text size and colors</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="tasks">Tasks</h2>
    <p>
      A task is something you want to do on a particular day. Today lists the day's tasks, with
      timed tasks in time order first and tasks without a time under "Any time".
    </p>
    <h3>Add or edit a task</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Only the title is required. You can also add a time (24-hour, for example 07:00), a date,
        and notes.
      </li>
      <li>The date starts as today. Tap a task on Today to edit or delete it.</li>
      <li>Deleting a task asks you to confirm first.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Things you can do from Today</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Complete</strong> marks a task done. You can undo it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tomorrow</strong> moves the task to the next day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dismiss</strong> sets a task aside without completing it. You can undo this too.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The app does not count streaks or show scores. If something cannot be saved, the app shows a
      message instead of failing quietly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="routines">Weekly routines</h2>
    <p>
      A routine repeats on a schedule, such as "Shower, 19:00, every day". Choose "Add a routine" on
      Today.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Enter a title, a time, a start date, and optional notes.</li>
      <li>
        Choose the weekdays it repeats on, or tap "Every day". Each day button shows a check mark
        and a label, so it does not rely on color. You need at least one day.
      </li>
      <li>
        A routine keeps the same local clock time, for example 19:00, when daylight-saving time
        changes or you change time zones.
      </li>
      <li>A routine appears under "Routines" on Today on the days it applies.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Managing your routines</h3>
    <p>
      Choose "Manage routines" on Today or in Settings to see every routine with its time and
      weekdays.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>"Edit" opens the whole routine.</li>
      <li>
        "Pause" hides a routine from Today and stops its reminders; "Resume" brings it back from
        today on. Days missed while paused are not filled in.
      </li>
      <li>
        "Delete" asks you to confirm first. It removes the routine and the changes you made to
        single days, and it cannot be undone.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Changing one day or the whole routine</h3>
    <p>When you tap a routine on Today, the two kinds of change are kept apart:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>This day only.</strong> Change the title, time, date, or notes with "Save for this
        day only", or choose "Skip this day". "Restore this day" and "Undo changes for this day"
        bring it back. The routine itself stays the same.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The whole routine.</strong> "Edit the whole routine" opens the routine. Days you
        changed individually keep their changes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On a day with a change, Today shows a short note at the top under "What changed today", such
      as "Swimming moved to 17:00 today" or "Swimming is skipped today", so a change is never a
      surprise.
    </p>
    <h2 id="reminders">Optional reminders</h2>
    <p>
      Reminders are local notifications created by the app on your device. They are off by default
      and do not need an account.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Turn reminders on for the device with "Turn on reminders" (on Today, once, or in Settings).
        Your phone then asks for permission. If you decline, the planner still works, and Settings
        shows the status and a button to open your device settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        Then choose per item: a task or routine has a "Remind me" checkbox, off unless you tick it.
        A task needs a time to have a reminder. You can pick "At the time" or up to 1 hour before.
      </li>
      <li>
        In Settings you can set the suggested notice for new items, the snooze length (5 minutes to
        1 hour), quiet hours, sound, and vibration. A reminder that falls in quiet hours is delayed
        until they end, or skipped if that would be after the task. On iPhone, vibration follows
        your device settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        A notification has a Snooze button that reminds you once more. Editing or deleting a task or
        routine updates its scheduled reminders.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="weather">Weather and location</h2>
    <p>
      Weather is optional and <strong>off by default</strong>. Until you turn it on, the app makes
      no weather requests and shows "Weather is off". The planner never waits for weather.
    </p>
    <h3>Turning it on</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        In Settings, the Weather card explains what is shared before anything is requested. Then
        choose one of two ways to give a place:
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use my device location.</strong> The app asks for location permission while it is in
        use, never in the background. It takes one approximate fix when a forecast is needed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Search for a city or postcode.</strong> Type at least three characters and press
        Search. Only your search text is sent, and no device location is needed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Coordinates are rounded to about 1 km before they are saved or sent. "Remove location" or
        turning weather off deletes the saved place and forecast from the device.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What Today shows</h3>
    <p>
      At the top of Today: an icon, conditions and temperature (including feels like, the day's high
      and low, and chance of precipitation). The clothing ideas come next, then your tasks. If the
      forecast is old, the app says so and shows how old. If there is no connection, no permission,
      or the provider has a problem, a short message explains it with a Try again or settings
      option, and your tasks stay available.
    </p>
    <p>
      Forecasts come from <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>, which receives the
      rounded coordinates (or your search text) and, like any website, your device's network
      address. The <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> page has more.
    </p>

    <h2 id="clothing">Clothing preferences</h2>
    <p>
      Clothing ideas appear on Today only when weather is on and a forecast is available. They are
      informational suggestions, not advice, and they use choices you make yourself.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        In Settings, the "Clothing suggestions" card lists items grouped as Tops, Bottoms,
        One-piece, Outerwear, Footwear, and Accessories.
      </li>
      <li>
        For each item choose "May suggest" or "Never suggest". "Never suggest" takes priority.
        "Reset clothing choices" returns everything to "May suggest".
      </li>
      <li>
        The app does not guess anything about you. Items are described by name only, and your
        choices stay on your device.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each idea comes with a short reason, such as the day's forecast high or the chance of rain.
        Tap Dismiss to hide an idea for the rest of that day.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Comfort varies. Suggestions may not reflect indoor conditions or what you personally need.
    </p>

    <h2 id="appearance">Text size and colors</h2>
    <p>
      In Settings, Appearance lets you pick the text size (four steps), compact or comfortable
      spacing, and a color theme: match your device, light, dark, or sage, plum, or clay accents.
      "Reset" restores the defaults. These choices are saved on your device.
    </p>

    <h2 id="feedback">Ideas and problems</h2>
    <p>
      You can suggest a feature or report a problem from inside the app. The app never asks you for
      feedback, so it is only sent when you choose to write.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        In Settings, under Feedback, choose "Suggest a feature or improvement" or "Report a
        problem". You can switch between Idea, Improvement, and Problem at the top of the form.
      </li>
      <li>
        For an idea, say what you would like the app to do and when it would help. For a problem,
        say what happened, and use "What were you trying to do?" if it helps explain.
      </li>
      <li>
        "Include device details" adds the app version, your phone's system and model, and your text
        size and color settings. The form shows exactly what will be added, and you can turn it off.
        Your tasks, routines, location, and clothing choices are never added.
      </li>
      <li>
        "Open in mail app" opens an email addressed to{' '}
        {supportContact.href ? (
          <a href={supportContact.href}>{supportContact.value}</a>
        ) : (
          <strong>{supportContact.value}</strong>
        )}
        . Nothing is sent until you press Send in your mail app. If you leave the screen first, what
        you wrote is kept on your device for next time.
      </li>
      <li>
        If no mail app is set up, the app shows the address with a "Copy address" button so you can
        write from any email account. The <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page has the same address.
      </li>
    </ul>
    {supportContact.isPlaceholder && (
      <p class="note">
        <strong>Not live yet.</strong> This address has been chosen, but its mailbox is not set up
        yet, so please do not send messages to it.
      </p>
    )}

    <p class="note">
      plank.to is a planning tool. It does not provide medical, therapeutic, or safety advice.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
